{-
   ==========================================================================================
   What a test binary was asked to do - Design-Test.md §5.3 and §6.
   ==========================================================================================

   The switches, and each one is the whole of a section rather than a convenience:

       --list                 print one qualified name per case and stop
       --filter <text>        only the cases whose qualified name contains this
       --only <name>          exactly one case, by qualified name - what `--isolate` re-executes as
       --shard <i>/<n>        the cases whose ordinal is `i` modulo `n` - §6
       --isolate              one child per case, rather than every case in this process - §5.3
       --jobs <n>             run `n` shards as children, in parallel - §6
       --timeout <ms>         kill a case that runs longer than this, and report it as a timeout
       --allow-empty          a selection that matched nothing is not a failure
       --update               rewrite a snapshot whose text has changed - §7's rulings 1 and 2
       --no-update            write nothing, and fail on a snapshot that is missing - what CI passes
       --prune                with --update, delete the snapshots a full run did not reach
       --snapshot-dir <path>  where the snapshots live, rather than `__snapshots__`
       --color <when>         `auto`, `always` or `never` - what the report is painted with
       --ascii                draw the report with no characters a terminal might not have
       --no-progress          no line that rewrites itself, on a terminal that would take one
       -v                     one line per case on the way past
       -q                     no summary either

   **A selection that matched nothing is a failure**, which is `test/README.md`'s empty-corpus guard
   said in the new framework's terms: a driver that verified nothing must not be mistaken for one
   that verified everything, and `--filter Mpa` matching zero cases is exactly that mistake. It is
   waived by a flag rather than by silence - `--allow-empty` - so that a shard with nothing in it is
   still expressible.

   `--shard i/n` is round-robin rather than contiguous, for the reason `test/shard.h` gives: the
   fixtures are not equal in cost and the expensive ones are not evenly spread through the listing.

   **The three presentation switches only ever override the detection downwards, with one exception.**
   `Test.Console` asks the stream what it is - see Terminal.yana - and a redirected run therefore
   sends no escape sequences without being asked. `--ascii` and `--no-progress` narrow that further;
   `--color always` is the one that widens it, and it exists because a program piped into a pager is
   the case detection provably cannot get right.

   An unrecognized switch is refused rather than ignored. A test harness that quietly did nothing
   because of a typo in a flag is the failure mode this whole document is about.

   **`--update` and `--no-update` are three states rather than two flags**, which is why the runner
   reads them into one number. A development run creates a snapshot it has never seen and *fails* on
   one that changed; `--update` rewrites the change; `--no-update` writes nothing at all and fails on
   the absence too. Writing them as an independent pair would let a command line say "update and do
   not update", which is not one of the three.
-}

{-
   What `--color` was given, as the three answers rather than as a pair of flags.

   `auto` is the detection - Terminal.yana's - and is what nobody has to write. The other two are the
   two ways it can be wrong: a pipe into a pager that renders sequences, and a terminal whose colours
   are unreadable in somebody's theme.
-}
pub data ColorChoice = ColorAuto | ColorAlways | ColorNever

pub data Options {
    list: Bool,
    filter: String,
    only: String,
    shard: Int,
    shards: Int,
    isolate: Bool,
    jobs: Int,
    timeoutMs: Int,
    allowEmpty: Bool,
    update: Bool,
    noUpdate: Bool,
    prune: Bool,
    snapshotDir: String,
    verbose: Bool,
    quiet: Bool,
    color: ColorChoice,
    ascii: Bool,
    noProgress: Bool,
    complaint: String
}

pub fn defaultOptions() -> Options = Options {
    list: False, filter: "", only: "", shard: 0, shards: 1, isolate: False, jobs: 1,
    timeoutMs: 0, allowEmpty: False, update: False, noUpdate: False, prune: False, snapshotDir: "",
    verbose: False, quiet: False, color: ColorAuto, ascii: False, noProgress: False, complaint: ""
}

{-
   The command line, read into the record above.

   `arguments` is everything after the program's own name. A switch that takes a value takes it as
   the next argument rather than after an `=`, because that is the form every driver in this tree
   already uses and there is nothing to gain by having two.
-}
pub fn readOptions(arguments: [String]) -> Options:
    let &options = defaultOptions()
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < length(arguments) && length(options.complaint) == (0 :: Size):
        let argument = arguments[i]
        i = i + 1

        {-
           One arm per spelling rather than one per switch, which is what a `match` on the word costs
           and what it buys: an alias is a row of the table rather than an `||` inside a condition,
           and the flags read here as the list at the top of this file writes them.

           The switches that take a value take it in the arm, through `takeValue` - which reads the
           next argument, steps the cursor over it and complains if there is none, so an arm is the
           one line that says what the value is for.
        -}
        match argument:
            "--list"         -> options.list = True
            "-list"          -> options.list = True
            "--isolate"      -> options.isolate = True
            "-isolate"       -> options.isolate = True
            "--allow-empty"  -> options.allowEmpty = True
            "--update"       -> options.update = True
            "-update"        -> options.update = True
            "--no-update"    -> options.noUpdate = True
            "--prune"        -> options.prune = True
            "--ascii"        -> options.ascii = True
            "--no-progress"  -> options.noProgress = True
            "-v"             -> options.verbose = True
            "--verbose"      -> options.verbose = True
            "-q"             -> options.quiet = True
            "--quiet"        -> options.quiet = True

            "--snapshot-dir" -> options.snapshotDir = takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument)
            "--filter"       -> options.filter = takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument)
            "-filter"        -> options.filter = takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument)
            "--only"         -> options.only = takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument)
            "-only"          -> options.only = takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument)
            "--color"        -> readColor(options, takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument))
            "--colour"       -> readColor(options, takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument))
            "--shard"        -> readShard(options, takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument))
            "-shard"         -> readShard(options, takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument))
            "--jobs"         -> options.jobs = readCount(options, takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument), argument)
            "-jobs"          -> options.jobs = readCount(options, takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument), argument)
            "-j"             -> options.jobs = readCount(options, takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument), argument)
            "--timeout"      -> options.timeoutMs = readCount(options, takeValue(options, arguments, i, argument), argument)

            -- A bare word is the filter, which is the shorthand every driver in `test/` already
            -- takes. A word starting with `-` is not: an unrecognized switch is a mistake, and
            -- taking it for a filter would silently run nothing.
            _:
                if length(argument) > (0 :: Size) && argument.unit(0) == "-":
                    complain(options, "unknown switch {argument}")
                else:
                    options.filter = copy(argument)

    -- The three states, checked once rather than per snapshot. A command line that asked for both
    -- would otherwise take whichever the runner tested first, which is the kind of answer nobody
    -- can predict from reading the flag.
    if options.update && options.noUpdate:
        complain(options, "--update and --no-update ask for opposite things - one rewrites a changed snapshot and the other writes nothing at all")

    if options.prune && !options.update:
        complain(options, "--prune deletes the snapshots a run did not reach, so it needs --update - a run that writes nothing should not be deleting anything")

    return options

{-
   The first complaint, and only the first.

   Every one of them stops the run, so the one worth printing is the one that came first: `--shard`
   at the end of the command line has no value to read, and the reader it would have been handed to
   would otherwise overwrite "needs a value" with a complaint about the shape of the empty string.
   Written once here rather than as a `length(options.complaint) == 0` in front of each site, which
   is what the two cross-checks above used to carry and what every caller kept forgetting.
-}
fn complain(&options: Options, ->why: String):
    if length(options.complaint) == (0 :: Size) then options.complaint = why

-- `auto`, `always` or `never`, and nothing else. A value this does not know is refused rather than
-- taken for `auto`, on the same terms an unrecognized switch is: `--color yes` doing nothing is how
-- somebody spends an afternoon wondering where the colour went.
fn readColor(&options: Options, value: String):
    match value:
        "auto"   -> options.color = ColorAuto
        "always" -> options.color = ColorAlways
        "force"  -> options.color = ColorAlways
        "never"  -> options.color = ColorNever
        "none"   -> options.color = ColorNever
        _        -> complain(options, "--color takes auto, always or never - not {value}")

-- The argument after the switch, with the cursor stepped over it. The empty string stands for the
-- one that was not there, and the complaint it left is what keeps a reader below from ruling on it.
fn takeValue(&options: Options, arguments: [String], &at: Size, switch: String) -> String:
    if at >= length(arguments):
        complain(options, "{switch} needs a value after it")
        return ""

    let value = copy(arguments[at])
    at = at + 1
    return value

-- `i/n`, which is what `run-tests.sh` already writes and what `shard.h` explains the shape of.
fn readShard(&options: Options, value: String):
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    match read(value, at) :: Result(ReadError, Int):
        Err(_) -> complain(options, "--shard takes <index>/<count>, as in --shard 0/8")
        Ok(index):
            if at >= length(value) || value.unit(at) != "/":
                complain(options, "--shard takes <index>/<count>, as in --shard 0/8")
            else:
                at = at + 1

                match read(value, at) :: Result(ReadError, Int):
                    Err(_) -> complain(options, "--shard takes <index>/<count>, as in --shard 0/8")
                    Ok(count):
                        if count <= 0 || index < 0 || index >= count:
                            complain(options, "--shard {index}/{count} names no shard - the index is from 0 and the count is above it")
                        else:
                            options.shard = index
                            options.shards = count

fn readCount(&options: Options, value: String, switch: String) -> Int:
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    if (read(value, at) :: Result(ReadError, Int)) is Ok(count):
        if count >= 0 then return count

    complain(options, "{switch} takes a number")
    return 0

{-
   Whether one case is in the selection.

   The three filters compose rather than override: `--filter Map --shard 1/4` is the `Map` cases of
   shard one, and the shard is applied to the *filtered* ordinal so that four shards of a filtered
   run still divide the work between them.
-}
pub fn selects(options: Options, name: String) -> Bool:
    if length(options.only) > (0 :: Size) then return name == options.only
    if length(options.filter) == (0 :: Size) then return True
    return String.contains(name, options.filter)
